71:To investigate brain regions with signi /uniFB01 cant extreme deviations, we further de /uniFB01 ned extreme deviations by thresholding the individualized deviation ( z value) derived from normative modeling using z of 6 2.6 (corresponding to p , .005 in a standard normal distribution) (Figure S1), as was performed in previous studies (42,63). A percentage map quantifying the proportion of individuals showing an extreme deviation was calculated for each group, separately for positive and negative extreme deviations. The proportion difference ( D percentage map) of individuals with extreme deviations was assessed between each diagnostic group with the HCtest group. A nonparametric group-based permutation test was conducted to identify signi /uniFB01 cant brain regions for each diagnostic group, and statistical signi /uniFB01 cance was set at .0167 (.05/3) with multiple comparisons correction based on the false discovery rate (FDR). The statistically signi /uniFB01 cant positive and negative extreme deviations were de /uniFB01 ned as supranormal and infranormal, respectively. To avoid reliance on a single threshold, the D percentage map of each group was validated based on a threshold-weighted calculation for de /uniFB01 ning extreme deviations, which integrated results across a range of thresholds (see the Supplement). Region-level deviations were embedded into large-scale ENs. A well-recognized framework of 4 ENs was revealed by a recent meta-analysis study (64), with distinct spatial distributions and functional pro /uniFB01 les ranging from emotional generation to regulation (EN 1: working memory and response inhibition; EN 2: cognitive appraisal and language processing of emotional stimuli; EN 3: emotion perception and generation; EN 4: emotional reactivity), and positioned on the DK atlas (Figure S2 and Table S1). The signi /uniFB01 cant networks were then evaluated for each diagnostic group using a groupbased permutation test at the regional level. The detai