67:region, which can be de /uniFB01 ned as a measurement of individualized alterations. Finally, to demonstrate the robustness of our model, we evaluated model generalizability and measurement stability. Model generalizability was assessed based on internal cross-validation, and external veri /uniFB01 cation was based on the HCtest group. Stability was validated by considering several potential confounding factors, including different strategies of brain parcellation and modeling method, as well as in /uniFB02 uences derived from medication (see the Supplement).
71:To investigate brain regions with signi /uniFB01 cant extreme deviations, we further de /uniFB01 ned extreme deviations by thresholding the individualized deviation ( z value) derived from normative modeling using z of 6 2.6 (corresponding to p , .005 in a standard normal distribution) (Figure S1), as was performed in previous studies (42,63). A percentage map quantifying the proportion of individuals showing an extreme deviation was calculated for each group, separately for positive and negative extreme deviations. The proportion difference ( D percentage map) of individuals with extreme deviations was assessed between each diagnostic group with the HCtest group. A nonparametric group-based permutation test was conducted to identify signi /uniFB01 cant brain regions for each diagnostic group, and statistical signi /uniFB01 cance was set at .0167 (.05/3) with multiple comparisons correction based on the false discovery rate (FDR). The statistically signi /uniFB01 cant positive and negative extreme deviations were de /uniFB01 ned as supranormal and infranormal, respectively. To avoid reliance on a single threshold, the D percentage map of each group was validated based on a threshold-weighted calculation for de /uniFB01 ning extreme deviations, which integrated results across a range of thresholds (see the Supplement). Region-level deviations were embedded into large-scale ENs. A well-recognized framework of 4 ENs was revealed by a recent meta-analysis study (64), with distinct spatial distributions and functional pro /uniFB01 les ranging from emotional generation to regulation (EN 1: working memory and response inhibition; EN 2: cognitive appraisal and language processing of emotional stimuli; EN 3: emotion perception and generation; EN 4: emotional reactivity), and positioned on the DK atlas (Figure S2 and Table S1). The signi /uniFB01 cant networks were then evaluated for each diagnostic group using a groupbased permutation test at the regional level. The details of the method can be found in the Supplement and Figure S3 and in previous studies (63).
79:The brainwide transcriptome was acquired from postmortem brain tissue samples of 6 neurotypical adult donors derived from the AHBA (http://human.brain-map.org) and preprocessed using the abagen toolbox (https://www.github.com/ netneurolab/abagen) following established protocols (65). The spatial correlations between the expression of each gene and neuroimaging deviations were calculated separately at the group and individual level. Two brain single-cell gene sequencing datasets were acquired from the Lake et al. (66) and Li et al. (67) cell-type gene markers. Seven cell types were adopted following a previous study (54), including astrocytes, endothelial cells, excitatory neurons, inhibitory neurons, microglia, oligodendrocytes, and oligodendrocyte precursors. The fast gene set enrichment analysis method was applied to perform cell-type enrichment analysis (68) and then obtained normalized enrichment scores (NESs) at the group and individual level, respectively. Pearson correlations were used to evaluate the relationship between individual NESs and affective symptoms in distinct disorders. More details are provided in the Supplement.
