emotional episodic memory using the Predictive Clinical Neuroscience toolkit (https://pcntoolkit.readthedocs.io/en/ latest). First, the task activation maps were used as response variables, and predictive factors comprised task performances including RT in the encoding and retrieval phase and ACC in the retrieval phase. We set the HCtrain group as the training sample to establish the normative reference, and the testing sample that comprised the clinical dataset (HCtest group) was positioned on the normative percentile charts for replicate validation and individualized evaluation. Next, we derived a z value from a model that quanti /uniFB01 ed the deviation from the normative reference for each participant in any given brain

region, which can be de /uniFB01 ned as a measurement of individualized alterations. Finally, to demonstrate the robustness of our model, we evaluated model generalizability and measurement stability. Model generalizability was assessed based on internal cross-validation, and external veri /uniFB01 cation was based on the HCtest group. Stability was validated by considering several potential confounding factors, including different strategies of brain parcellation and modeling method, as well as in /uniFB02 uences derived from medication (see the Supplement).

