Xiao2024.pdf.md:11:METHODS: We used normative modeling to establish a reference for functional activation of functional magnetic resonance imaging based on an emotional episodic memory task, which is frequently used to study affective symptoms in psychiatric disorders. This normative reference was derived from a large dataset of healthy individuals ( n = 409) and used to evaluate individualized functional alterations by calculating deviations from this reference in a clinical dataset, which included 164 healthy control participants and patients with major depressive disorder (MDD) ( n = 56), bipolar disorder (BD) ( n = 31), and schizophrenia (SZ) ( n = 73). The functional deviations were mapped to emotional networks (ENs) with speci /uniFB01 c emotional functions and used to predict affective symptoms in different mental disorders. The microscale cellular signatures underlying macroscale variations were identi /uniFB01 ed using imaging transcriptomic analysis and associated with affective symptoms.
Xiao2024.pdf.md:13:RESULTS: We observed distinct patterns of cross-scale neural alterations linked to affective symptoms in 3 psychiatric disorders. Macroscale neural dysfunctions in distinct disorders were embedded into non-overlapping ENs and signi /uniFB01 cantly associated with affective symptoms. Oligodendrocytes may mediate the network-speci /uniFB01 c impairments and microglia for MDD, astrocytes for BD, and excitatory neurons for SZ as replicable cell-type correlates of affective symptoms.
Xiao2024.pdf.md:21:Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) studies have been a major step forward in characterizing macroscale neural dysfunctions related to affective symptoms in psychiatric patients (5,10,11). The emotional episodic memory task (12 -14), a widely used paradigm, effectively captures functional activation associated with adaptive memory-enhancing effects induced by emotional stimuli (15 -17). Maladaptive alterations in this task have frequently been observed in major depressive disorder (MDD) (18,19), bipolar disorder (BD) (20,21), and schizophrenia (SZ) (22,23), suggesting the neural dysfunctions of affective perturbation. Disrupted emotional memory retrieval has been reported that may manifest in multiple processes of pathological emotion in psychiatric patients, involving disturbances in emotional attention (24), perceptions (25), and regulation (26). Importantly, these disruptions were thought to link to the vulnerability and development of speci /uniFB01 c clinical affective syndromes (27,28), underscoring the task ' s relevance in assessing affective symptoms.
Xiao2024.pdf.md:25:Studies using emotional episodic memory tasks have identi/uniFB01 ed altered neurofunctions related to affective symptoms in the amygdala and medial temporal lobe (MTL) system across various mental disorders (29 -31), suggesting heterogeneous patterns of neural dysfunctions in distinct diagnoses (18,32 -34). However, inconsistent results have often been reported, both within one speci /uniFB01 c diagnosis and across disorders. In MDD, increased activity in the amygdala and orbitofrontal gyrus has often been associated with emotional bias (18,19), but another study has shown no abnormalities (33). Hypoactivation in the amygdala and occipital gyrus has been recognized in patients with BD compared with patients with MDD (20,35), although an earlier study reported increased activity (36). Individuals with SZ have shown both hyperactivation and hypoactivation in the prefrontal and parietal cortices (37 -39), accompanied by coactivation with the MTL areas (37,38,40). Furthermore, while a study has reported higher activation of the cingulate cortex in patients with BD relative to patients with SZ (34), others have reported the opposite (41).
Xiao2024.pdf.md:31:In the current study, we aimed to investigate cross-scale neural dysfunctions related to affective symptoms in patients with MDD, BD, and SZ (Figure 1). By utilizing 2 task-fMRI datasets and a normative modeling framework, robust and individualized functional deviations during emotional memory retrieval were /uniFB01 rst identi /uniFB01 ed. Next, we embedded deviation patterns into macroscale emotional networks (ENs) to explore the functional annotations of emotional pro /uniFB01 ling associated with neural variability in distinct disorders and assessed the associations with affective symptoms dimension. Finally, we conducted transcriptomic association analysis to plausibly determine the cellular abnormalities linked to affective symptoms by integrating macroscale dysfunctions with microscale alterations from the perspective of brainwide transcriptome and brain single-cell sequencing. The normative modeling approach enables the disentangling of the cross-scale heterogeneous patterns of neural dysfunctions underlying affective symptoms within a single diagnosis while simultaneously identifying between-diagnoses commonalities and differences. Building on evidence of common disruption in emotional memory and explicit distinction of affective symptoms across different disorders, we hypothesized that individualized deviations would converge in limited regions but diverge signi /uniFB01 -cantly across large-scale networks between diagnoses.
Xiao2024.pdf.md:37:The participants of this study were drawn from 2 datasets. The healthy dataset included 409 healthy control (HC) participants (HCtrain group) recruited from the Center for MRI Research, Peking University. The clinical dataset consisted of 324 individuals: 56 individuals with MDD, 31 individuals with BD, 73 individuals with SZ, and 164 HC participants (HCtest group). These participants were recruited from the Center for Neuroimaging, Peking University Sixth Hospital. The study was approved by the medical ethics committee of Peking University Sixth Hospital (see the Supplement for details of the ethics reviews), and all participants provided written informed consent before taking part in the study. All psychiatric patients were assessed and diagnosed using DSM-IV criteria by experienced psychiatric physicians. The severity of depression in patients with MDD was assessed using the Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HAMD), while the clinical symptoms of BD were assessed using both the HAMD and the Young Mania Rating Scale. The symptoms of patients with SZ were evaluated using the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS). The HC participants had no current or lifetime history of Axis I psychiatric disorders or neurological disorders. See the Supplement for additional criteria for participants inclusion and exclusion and quality-control details.
Xiao2024.pdf.md:43:Figure 1. The work /uniFB02 ow of the current study design. (A) Data on functional activation for each participant under task -fMRI of emotional episodic memory were extracted from 2 research datasets (the healthy dataset and the clinical dataset). (B) The normative model was /uniFB01 rst established based on functional activation of each region as response variables and task performances as predicted covariates, drawing from the HCtrain group of the healthy dataset. The HCtest group from the clinical dataset was further put into the normative model to replicate validation, and individualized alterations were evaluated as deviations relative to the normative range in any given brain region for each patient in the clinical dataset. (C) Overview of analysis pipeline comprising 3 steps. Model generalizability was assessed based on the similarity between the HCtrain and HCtest groups; the stability of measurements derived from the normative reference was evaluated through the replicate validation step using different brain parcellation atlases and modeling methods and validated whether these measurements could be in /uniFB02 uenced by the medication effect (step 1). The macroscale alterations were quanti /uniFB01 ed as the differences between the proportion of individuals showing an extreme deviation of each case and HC participants at the regional level and further embedded into emotional network for speci /uniFB01 c functional annotations of emotional pro /uniFB01 ling. Individual deviations with signi /uniFB01 cant differences were used to predict affective symptoms of each psychiatric disorder (step 2). Transcriptomic analysis and cell-type enrichment were further applied to examine the microscale alterations related to macroscale variability and linked to affective symptoms in distinct disorders (step 3). BD, bipolar disorder; fMRI, functional magnetic resonance imaging; HC, healthy control; MDD, major depressive disorder; ROI, region of interest; SZ, schizophrenia.
Xiao2024.pdf.md:61:Figure 2. The emotional episodic memory task and generalizability evaluation of the normative model. (A) The emotional episodic memory task consisted of encoding and retrieval phases, and each phase included neutral and aversive scenes. The participants were instructed to determine whether each image represented an indoor or outdoor scene in the encoding phase (left) and to respond ' new ' or ' old ' in the retrieval phase (right). (B) The distribution of mean regional deviations in the HCtrain and HCtest groups. There was no signi /uniFB01 cant difference between these 2 distributions. (C) The spatial distribution of the regional deviation map in the HCtrain group exhibited signi /uniFB01 cant spatial correlations with the group-averaged activation and regional deviation maps in the HCtest group. HC, healthy control.
