Xiao2024.pdf.md:29:Complex clinical phenotypes of affective symptoms are not only associated with macroscale functional abnormalities but are also tied to alterations at the microscale genetic and cellular levels (47 -49). Studies of postmortem brain samples and animal models have consistently pointed toward cellular processes in the central nervous system, including pathological alterations of neurons and neuroglial cells, as important factors in the development of affective symptoms (50 -52). However, due to limitations in spatial resolution and coarse diagnostic classi /uniFB01 cations, the relationship between microscopic vulnerabilities, macroscale dysfunctions, and clinical phenotypes remains poorly understood. The Allen Human Brain Atlas (AHBA) microarray dataset has been utilized to investigate transcriptomic patterns linked to neuroimaging /uniFB01 ndings (53,54), with substantial evidence indicating a connection between gene expression and macroscale dysfunctions (55,56). The brainwide gene expression atlas has made it possible to bridge the gap between cross-scale functional alterations (57) and, by combining with a normative modeling framework, could identify the personalized multiscale associations of neural vulnerability.
Xiao2024.pdf.md:157:Several issues need to be considered. First, the clinical sample size was relatively small, especially for a transdiagnostic analysis, and a larger sample would help to better parse clinical heterogeneity and improve the overall robustness of the /uniFB01 ndings. Second, non-overlapping measures for affective symptoms were used across different disorders. A more comprehensive assessment would help better characterize the affective symptoms of different mental disorders. Additionally, data on depressive severity in patients with BD were partially missing, making it impossible to calculate the anxiety/somatization factor of the HAMD to represent affective symptoms in BD. Third, most patients with BD in this study were in the euthymic stage. Further study with more patients in different stages is imperative to assess the reproducibility of our /uniFB01 ndings. Fourth, medication has long been considered as an important confounding factor. Despite sensitivity analysis being conducted to assess the potential impact of medication on the results (Figure S7), the possibility of confounding effects from medication cannot be entirely ruled out. Future studies should include a larger sample of unmedicated participants to validate our /uniFB01 ndings. Fifth, gene expression data from the AHBA were obtained from 6 healthy donors but may not fully represent the variability in cortical gene expression among individuals. Although the transcriptome association analysis operates under the assumption that cortical gene expression patterns exhibit a high degree of consistency across individuals (57), genes with greater interindividual variability might not have been adequately captured, potentially limiting the statistical power of associations. This limitation should be carefully considered when interpreting the /uniFB01 ndings. Additionally, gene expression data from the left hemisphere may not fully represent macroscale dysfunctions of the entire brain. Finally, cell-type enrichment analyses were based on independent datasets for imaging and transcriptomic data, with their relationship being inferred indirectly through spatial variations, and future research incorporating within-subject designs will be essential to directly assess the link between genetic and neuroimaging data.
